{0 The Library Scope Model}

Two libraries can define modules with the same name. For example, [eliom.client] and
[eliom.server] both have an [Eliom__Content]. While it's never intended that a program
links with both of these libraries at the same time, it may be convenient for
{e documentation} to do this, and hence we end up in a situation where odoc needs
to distinguish between the two modules. In references, we have syntax to disambiguate
by specifying the library explicitly, but we may also end up traversing syntactic
elements in modules, and in that case we need to be very careful to ensure signature
items are treated as they are in OCaml.

An example might make this clearer. In both [eliom.client] and [eliom.server], there
is a module, [Eliom.Content] that is an alias of [Eliom__Content]. A reference
like [{!/eliom.client/Eliom.Content}] picks the correct module [Eliom] by using
the explicit library syntax. However, picking the correct [Eliom__Content] module
requires knowing which modules are in scope {e for the chosen Eliom module}, as
compiling with [-no-alias-deps] (as dune does) means that we cannot rely on digests
to uniquely identify the correct target of the aliases.

The scope is decided at compile time - each unit records the libraries it was built against, which the
driver passes with [-L] to [odoc compile] (see {{!page-driver}the driver
documentation}). This page gives the rules [odoc] follows to resolve a module
name with that information, and the assumptions those rules make about the
libraries and the driver. It is written for driver authors and for [odoc]
developers, and there are a number of scope tests that refer to its rules by name. It is a
work in progress -
{{!gaps}section 11} lists where [odoc] does not yet follow it.

The rules cover the names of modules, in interfaces and implementations. R1
({{!refs}section 4}) finds local items, so it applies to names of every kind.
Pages are not covered yet, nor are packs ([-pack]) and OxCaml parameterised
libraries ([-parameter], [-instantiate]), whose names are bound in ways these
rules do not describe.

The rules apply to [.cmt] units too, though [odoc] handles a few
constructs that can appear in an implementation less well (see
{{!page-driver}the driver documentation}).

The rules are for "sane" libraries (D3 and D4 in {{!assumptions}section 2}).
[odoc] need not give the right answer for all possible combinations of
modules.

The rules are not proved formally: there is no formal framework for the module
system to prove them in. Instead, {{!operations}section 7} lists every
operation [odoc] performs on names, with what each must keep, so that each can
be checked with a short argument.

{1:definitions 1. Definitions}

While we're not being rigorous, it's still helpful to have some definitions
to help describe the operations.

- A {e unit} [u] is one [.odoc] file made by [odoc compile] from a [.cmti],
  [.cmt] or [.cmi]. It has a name [name(u)] (a capitalised module name) and a
  digest [dg(u)]: the digest that other units record when they import
  [name(u)], which is the digest of its [.cmi]. Not every unit is part of a
  library, but the units this model is about are, and [lib(u)] is the library
  of [u]. A unit made
  by [odoc compile-impl] follows the same rules, but it is never a candidate
  for a name: names mean interfaces.
- A {e library} is a name, bound to one directory of units (we will ignore
  archives for the purposes of this discussion). Within one
  library, no two units have the same name. Drivers can bind several names to
  one directory, and the units of that directory then belong to each of them,
  which is harmless under D3. This lets the [.odoc] directories mirror the
  compiler's. The [compiler-libs.*] libraries share one directory of [.cmi]
  files, so a unit built against [compiler-libs.common] can use a module of
  [compiler-libs.bytecomp], as the compiler allows. With one [.odoc]
  directory bound to all those names, [odoc] finds that module too.
  [odoc link] checks by default that no two [-L] directories overlap, so a
  driver that shares a directory passes [--custom-layout], which turns the
  check off.
- [Λc(u)] is the set of library names passed with [-L] when [u] was
  compiled. [odoc] records it in the unit. A unit compiled with no [-L] is a
  {e legacy unit}, and has no [Λc].
- [Λl] is the set of library names passed with [-L] to [odoc link]. At link,
  [Λl] also gives the directory of each library named in any [Λc]. Where
  this page says a name is looked up in [Λl], [odoc] looks along its whole
  search path, which also has the [-I] directories.
- [units(L, N)] is the set of units named [N] in the libraries [L], not
  counting units made by [odoc compile-impl].
- [u] {e records digest [d] for [N]} if the import table of [u]'s compiled
  file has the entry [(N, d)]. It {e records no digest for [N]} if the table
  has an entry for [N] with no digest, as a pure alias compiled with
  [-no-alias-deps] gives. The table belongs to the unit, not to each
  individual occurrence of [N]. The compiler adds an entry for every name that a [.cmi]
  it loads records with a digest, so a unit can record a digest for a name
  it never used itself. A name recorded with no digest is not copied: a unit
  that includes a pure alias has no entry for the alias's target.
- An {e implementation copy} is the interface of a virtual module of a
  virtual library, documented again in the library of one implementation
  of it ({{!virtual_libraries}section 3.5}).
- A unit is {e hidden} if its name contains [__], as dune's [X__M] does.
  Hidden units are not linked to (S3).
- An alias [module M = X__M] is to a {e self-canonical} module when the
  canonical path of [X__M], from its [@canonical] tag, is the alias itself,
  as for dune's alias in [X] with [@canonical X.M]. [odoc] then shows the
  signature of [X__M] at [M].

Two kinds of name appear in a unit:

- A {e path} is a name in the typed tree, such as the [A] in [module X = A]
  or in [A.t]. The compiler bound its root to a unit, except for a pure alias
  compiled with [-no-alias-deps], whose root the compiler did not look up.
- A {e reference} is a name written in a doc comment, such as [{!A}], or the
  target of a [@canonical] tag. The compiler never sees it. It is written by
  hand, and it may name things the compiler could not see when the unit was
  compiled.

The two kinds follow different rules: {{!paths}section 3} for paths,
{{!refs}section 4} for references. {{!display}Section 5} says how names are
shown, which is separate from what they mean.

{1:assumptions 2. Assumptions}

The rules are only correct if these conditions hold. D3 and D4 are about the
libraries being documented. The others are about the driver. A driver that
passes no [-L] at compile produces legacy units ({{!legacy}section 6}).

- {b D1 (complete scope).} [Λc(u)] includes [lib(u)]. If [u] records a
  digest [d] for [N], the unit with name [N] and digest [d] is in a library
  in [Λc(u)].
  Directories given to the compiler with [-H] count. A library in [Λc(u)]
  holds a unit for each of its interfaces, or the driver leaves the library
  out of [Λc(u)].
- {b D2 (closure).} If [Λc(u)] includes [lib(v)], it includes all of [Λc(v)].
  So all units of one library have the same [Λc]. This must hold across
  separate runs of the driver. It assumes transitive dependencies are visible,
  as dune does by default. [(implicit_transitive_deps false)] is not covered
  for now.
- {b D3 (sane libraries).} No [Λc(u)] holds two units of the same name,
  except a virtual library's unit and its implementation copies. A [Λc] that
  is larger than what the compiler could see is fine, as long as it meets
  D3.
- {b D4 (aliases look back).} The target of a pure alias in a unit of
  library [l] is a unit of [l], or of a library [l] depends on. It is never a
  unit of a library compiled later.
- {b D5 (order).} If [u] records a digest for [N], the unit it imported was
  compiled with [odoc compile] before [u] was, and its [.odoc] file is
  complete when [u] reads it. This holds for [odoc compile-impl] too. It
  matters only for resolution at compile ({{!operations}section 7}).
- {b D6 (link scope).} At the link of [u], for every unit [o] whose paths are
  resolved, [Λl] includes all of [Λc(o)]. That covers [u], the units whose
  terms appear in [u]'s output, and the units reached by projecting a
  reference. An implementation read only to find a source link has no paths
  resolved, so it is not covered. Each library in [Λl] holds the [.odoc] file
  of each of its units.
  [odoc] checks the first part and warns about each library that is missing.
  It cannot check the second part.
- {b D7 (one directory).} A library name means the same directory in every
  run of [odoc] that reads a unit recording that name.
- {b D8 (implementations).} A unit made by [odoc compile-impl] has the same
  [Λc] as the interface of the same module.
- {b D9 (copies).} The driver documents an implementation copy in the
  implementation's library, with the implementation of that module, made by
  [odoc compile-impl], next to it. The copy's [Λc] is the implementation's.
  The other units that an implementation's directory holds copies of (the
  virtual library's alias module, and its modules that are not virtual) are
  not documented again. The driver knows which interfaces it copies, so a
  later version may let it mark a copy instead. (See {{!gaps}section 11}.)

{1:paths 3. Paths}

{2:roots 3.1 Roots and Origins}

The head of a path is either a {e root} (a unit name) or a {e local}
identifier (bound by an enclosing signature, a functor parameter, and so
on). A local is looked up in the enclosing signatures and parameters that
bind it; no library is searched. Its definition can still contain roots, as
in [module N = Other], and those follow the rules below. Once a root is
resolved to an identifier, no library needs to be searched for it again. The
rules below are for unresolved roots.

Every occurrence of a root has an {e origin}: the unit whose source it came
from.

- A root in a unit's typed tree has that unit as its origin.
- When [odoc] copies a term from one unit into another (by expanding it,
  including it, substituting it, or following an alias to it), every root in
  it keeps its origin. [odoc] recomputes the signatures of [include] and
  [module type of] from the unit they name, and drops the compiler's copy,
  so the roots in them have the origin of that unit.
- A root that [odoc] makes from another occurrence, for example when it
  strengthens a path, takes that occurrence's origin.
- A root the compiler made and [odoc] does not recompute, such as one in the
  inferred type of a value in a [.cmt] with no [.mli], has as its origin the
  unit whose compiled file holds it. That unit's import table has the entry,
  because the compiler loaded the [.cmi] the type came from.

If [odoc] cannot recompute a signature, it warns and keeps the compiler's.
This is sometimes unavoidable: dune compiles [.odoc] files only for the
libraries in its workspace, never for installed ones such as [Stdlib]. So
[odoc] cannot find the unit a signature names when that unit is installed,
as in [module type of Stdlib.List], and keeping the compiler's signature is
better than leaving items out. [odoc] also need not recompute [module type of
F(M)], or other signatures of a functor application. It may keep the
compiler's, with the warning.
The roots and comments in that signature are outside the claims of
{{!claims}section 3.7}. The warning is an ordinary one, unless the failure is
a missing root, which {{!path_failures}section 3.6} reports. (See
{{!gaps}section 11} for [module type of] a functor application.)

One term can hold roots of several origins. For example, after [F(B)] is
applied in [u], the roots from the body of [F] have the origin of the unit
that defines [F], and [B] has the origin [u].

{2:meaning 3.2 Meaning}

The meaning [⟦N⟧] of a root occurrence [N] with origin [o] is:

{ul
{li {b P1 (digest).} If [o] records a digest [d] for [N], the candidates
    [C] are the units in [units(Λc(o), N)] whose digest is [d].}
{li {b P2 (no digest).} If [o] records no digest for [N], the candidates
    [C] are all the units in [units(Λc(o), N)].}
{li {b P3 (choice).} If P1 or P2 gives candidates [C] and [C] holds
    exactly one unit, [⟦N⟧] is that unit. If V1 applies
    ({{!virtual_libraries}section 3.5}), [⟦N⟧] is the unit it picks.
    Otherwise the root is unresolved. This covers:
    {ul
    {li a digest that no unit matches, for example because a library is
        missing at link;}
    {li no unit named [N];}
    {li several candidates, which D3 rules out except for virtual
        libraries;}
    {li a root that is not in [o]'s import table at all. The compiler
        records every unit it looks up, so this does not happen for a root
        of [o]'s typed tree. No rule can give such a root a meaning.}}}}

P2 applies only when no digest is recorded. A digest that fails to match
never falls back to a lookup by name: that could pick a unit with the right
name and the wrong contents.

[⟦N⟧] depends only on [N] and [o]. It does not depend on the unit being
linked, on where the term is shown, or on [Λl], except that [Λl] gives the
directories.

The meaning of a whole path is the meaning of its root, followed by
{e projection}: looking up each next component in the signature of the
module meant so far, expanding it where needed. When projection meets a
path, for example the target of an alias or a module type path, the roots
in that path are resolved by these same rules, with their own origins.

{2:late_aliases 3.3 Aliases Bound Late}

A pure alias compiled with [-no-alias-deps] is not bound by the unit that
writes it. Each unit that uses it binds it again, through its own [-I]. For
example:

{t
  | File         | Contents             | Built against                        |
  |--------------|----------------------|--------------------------------------|
  | [ext1/ext.mli] | [type t = A]       | nothing                              |
  | [ext2/ext.mli] | [type t = B]       | nothing                              |
  | [l/l.mli]      | [module M = Ext]   | [ext1], with [-no-alias-deps]        |
  | [c/c.mli]      | [val x : L.M.t]    | [l], [ext1] and [ext2]               |
}

[l] records no digest for [Ext]. To check [L.M.t], the compiler loads an
[Ext] for [c]: the first one in [-I] order. If that is [ext2]'s, [c] binds
the alias to a unit its author never saw, and P2 gives the wrong unit on
[c]'s page.

This needs two libraries that define the same top-level module in one
unit's scope, which D3 rules out. dune compiles such a setup, for example
with two [(wrapped false)] libraries, but a program links only while [l]'s
dependency on [ext1] is dead. If [l] itself uses its [Ext], linking a
program that uses both fails with "Duplicated implementations". Under D3 and
D4, every unit that uses the alias has only one unit of that name in its
scope, the author's target, and so binds the alias as its author did. If D3
is broken, [odoc] may link the author's choice where a later unit took
another one. [odoc] does not try to detect this.

{2:candidates 3.4 Several Candidates}

The compiler does not reject two units of one name in its [-I] path. At
[-c], for interfaces and implementations, it binds the name to the first
match in [-I] order. [odoc] does not record that order. Under D3 this does
not happen, except for virtual libraries. If it does, P3 leaves the root
unresolved when the candidates cannot be told apart by digest.

{2:virtual_libraries 3.5 Virtual Libraries}

dune compiles every implementation of a virtual library [vl] against the one
[.cmi] of each virtual module. A driver documents that interface several
times: once in [vl], with no source links, and once more in each
implementation, as an implementation copy with source links to that
implementation. The copies have the same name and digest.

- {b V1.} Suppose the candidates [C] of P1 or P2
  ({{!meaning}section 3.2}) have one name and one digest, so they are one
  interface documented more than once. A candidate is a copy if its library
  holds an implementation of that module (D9). If exactly one candidate is a
  copy, it is the meaning. Otherwise, if exactly one candidate is not a
  copy, it is [vl]'s unit, and it is the meaning. Otherwise the root is
  unresolved.

So a root whose origin depends on [vl] alone has only [vl]'s unit in [C],
and links to [vl]'s page. A root whose origin depends on one implementation
has that implementation's copy in [C] too, and links to the copy, whose
source links are right for it. dune forbids two implementations of one
library in one closure. If a driver lets that happen, [vl]'s page is still
correct.

At compile, the implementation next to a copy may not be compiled yet. V1
then finds no copy and two other candidates, so the root is left for link,
when every implementation has been compiled.

The choice shows only where a root names the virtual module's unit itself.
For a wrapped [vl], that unit is hidden, and the usual forms, such as
[Vl.M.t], go through [vl]'s alias module, whose origin is in [vl]. They
reach [vl]'s unit whatever the implementation. The choice shows for a [vl]
with [(wrapped false)], and in roots the compiler made
({{!roots}section 3.1}), such as those that source links follow.

References choose between the copies by the libraries given at link
instead ({{!lookup}section 4.2}, R3).

A source link does not need the interface to be resolved. It follows
shapes, which name units, from the unit being linked, and takes the
implementation of each name among that unit's libraries
({{!operations}section 7}). An implementation is chosen by the closure that
uses it, not where the virtual library's alias is written. dune forbids two
implementations of one virtual library in a closure, even a library's, so
those libraries hold at most one. So the link is right even when V1 cannot
choose.

{2:path_failures 3.6 Reporting Failures}

A failure is classified by the innermost root that fails.

- A {e missing root} is one where no unit of the searched libraries has the
  name [N]. On a path, the compiler checked the name, so this means a
  library was not given. That is already reported by the D6 check, so a
  missing root is reported again only with [--enable-missing-root-warning].
  A missing root takes precedence over a digest that no unit matches.
- {e Any other failure} gets an ordinary warning: several candidates, a
  digest no unit matches, or a root that resolves while the rest of the path
  does not.

This is for paths only. {{!ref_failures}Section 4.5} says what happens for
references.

{2:claims 3.7 Guarantees}

These claims are about roots whose origin is not a legacy unit, and that are
not in a signature [odoc] kept from the compiler ({{!roots}section 3.1}).

- {b Soundness.} Under D1 to D9, if P1 applies, [⟦N⟧] is the interface the
  compiler checked [o] against, or an implementation copy of it (V1). If P2
  applies, [⟦N⟧] is the target the author of the alias meant, and every unit
  that uses the alias binds it the same way. Otherwise the root is
  unresolved, and is reported as {{!path_failures}section 3.6} says.
- {b Completeness.} Under D1 to D9, every root resolves. A root fails only
  when an assumption is broken: a library it needs is missing at link (D6),
  or two units have its name and digest without being copies of one
  interface (D3).

The argument for soundness is in two parts:

{ol
{li Given its origin, a root gets the right unit.
    {ul
    {li Under P1, by D1 the unit [o] imported is in a library in [Λc(o)],
        and the digest picks it out. By D3, no other unit of that name is
        there, except implementation copies, which V1 handles. A unit that
        shows the term later cannot change this: the term was checked when
        [o] was compiled.}
    {li Under P2, by D4 the target is in [Λc(o)], and by D3 it is the only
        unit of that name there, except implementation copies, which V1
        handles. By D2 and D3, the same holds in the scope of
        each unit that uses the alias, so they all bind it the same way.}
    {li By D6 and D7, [Λc(o)] can be read at link, from the directories the
        compiler used.}}}
{li Every operation in {{!operations}section 7} keeps the origin of every
    root.}}

So at link, a root is looked up by name in its origin's [Λc]. A root with a
digest was resolved when its origin was compiled (D5), unless V1 left it for
link. If one is left, by D3 the lookup by name finds the unit its digest
names, or a virtual library's unit and its copies, between which V1
chooses. The digests of the unit
being linked are only about its own library's roots, and they are used only
there, so that a digest that matches no unit is not looked up by name.

Paths are never ambiguous: by D3, [Λc(o)] holds one unit of each name,
except the copies V1 chooses between. Only references and canonical tags
({{!refs}section 4}) can be ambiguous. There is no order of lookup that
settles an ambiguity; the author writes [{!/library/N}].

{1:refs 4. References}

{2:ref_origin 4.1 Origin}

Every doc comment has an origin: the unit whose source it came from, by the
same rules as for roots ({{!roots}section 3.1}). In particular, the comments
in a recomputed [include] are those of the unit it names, with their
bindings from R0.

{2:lookup 4.2 Lookup}

A reference [{!N}], or the first part of a dotted reference [{!N.x}], is
looked up in this order:

{ul
{li {b R1 (local).} In the signature that encloses the comment, and the
    functor parameters in scope there, then outwards, in the unit where the
    comment was written. In this signature, [{!M}] means its own [M], even if
    several units are called [M]:

    {[
    module M : sig end
    type t = int (** {!M} *)
    ]}

    R1 looks only for the kinds the reference allows: all kinds for
    [{!M}], one kind for a typed reference such as [{!module-M}], and
    modules and module types before a dot, as in [{!M.x}]. A local of an
    allowed kind stops the lookup, so R3 is not tried. If locals of two
    allowed kinds match, the reference is ambiguous, and the author picks
    one by giving its kind. A typed reference is also how the author reaches
    a root that a local of another kind hides: if [M] is a local module
    type, [{!M}] means it, and [{!module-M}] means the root module.

    Known limit: in OCaml only a module can come before a dot, so a module
    type never hides a module there. [odoc] also lets a reference look
    inside a module type, as in [{!S.x}], so before a dot a local module
    type [M] hides a root module [M], and [{!M.x}] does not reach the root.

    R1 sees the whole of each enclosing signature, including items declared
    after the comment. If a signature binds one name twice in one kind, as
    two values may be, or as an [include] and a later item may, R1 picks
    the last binding, which is the one the signature exports. The compiler
    rejects two modules, or two module types, of one name in a
    signature. A functor parameter is not in scope in its own
    signature, as in OCaml.

    R1 does not see names brought in by an [open] in a signature. After
    [open Foo], the author writes [{!Foo.t}], not [{!t}]. This may change
    later. If it does, some references that now resolve as roots will
    resolve locally.}
{li {b R2 (initial open).} In the modules given to [odoc] with [--open], the
    later ones first. The default is [Stdlib], so [{!List}] means
    [Stdlib.List]. These modules are found as roots by R3. This is how
    [--open] already works.

    Known limit: [odoc] does not see the compiler's own [-open] flags, such
    as the [-open Lib__] of a dune wrapped library
    ({{!page-odoc_for_authors.reference_scope}Reference Scope} says what
    this means for authors). [--open] is also given per run,
    not recorded in the unit, so a comment shown on another page uses that
    page's opens. (See {{!gaps}section 11} for the wrapper's page.)}
{li {b R3 (root).} Otherwise [N] is a root. If the origin of the
    reference is a unit named [N], [N] means that unit: the compiler never
    binds a unit's own name inside it, so no other unit of that name can be
    meant there. Otherwise its candidates are [units(Λl, N)]. If there is
    exactly one, that is its meaning. If they are a virtual library [vl]'s
    unit and implementation copies of it, told apart as in V1
    ({{!virtual_libraries}section 3.5}), and exactly one candidate is a
    copy, that copy is the meaning: a virtual library stands for an
    implementation not yet chosen, and once one is chosen, a reference
    links to it, with its source links. If several candidates are copies,
    [vl]'s unit is the meaning. Otherwise it is unresolved.
    [{!/library/N}] restricts the candidates to [units({library}, N)]. If
    [library] is not in [Λl], it is unresolved. (See {{!gaps}section 11} for
    a root with the name of the unit being linked.)}}

R3 looks in [Λl], not in [Λc(o)]. This allows forward references: a comment
can name a unit of a library compiled later, if it is given at link. If a
unit of [Λc(o)] and a unit of a library added at link have the same name,
the reference is ambiguous, and the author picks one with [{!/library/N}].
A virtual library's unit and its copies are the exception: R3 chooses
between them.

Known limit: a comment shown on several pages is resolved with the [Λl] of
each page. If those differ, a root reference can mean different units on
different pages.

Known limit: paths choose between the copies of a virtual library by
[Λc(o)] (V1), and references by [Λl] (R3). So in a unit built against [vl]
alone, a path to the virtual module's unit goes to [vl]'s page, while a
reference to it goes to the copy when [Λl] holds one implementation. The
two pages differ only in their source links.

The rest of a dotted reference is resolved by projection, as for a path
({{!meaning}section 3.2}). Paths met on the way follow {{!paths}section 3},
with their own origins.

{2:when 4.3 When}

- {b R0.} When a unit is compiled, R1 binds the first part of each reference
  in that unit's own comments, before any expansion can move the comment.
  The result is stored as an identifier. The rest of a dotted reference is
  resolved by projection at link. So at link, no reference needs the
  context it was written in. (See {{!gaps}section 11}.)

{2:copies 4.4 Copies}

{ul
{li {b R4 (copies).} From then on, a reference that R1 bound to a local item
    is treated as a local path to that item:
    {ul
    {li if an operation copies the item (by [include], by expansion, by
        [with type t = …] or by [with module M = A]), the reference names the
        copy;}
    {li if an operation replaces the item by a path (a functor argument, or
        [with module M := A]), the reference becomes that path, and
        {{!paths}section 3} gives it meaning. Its origin is the unit where
        the application or the [with] was written;}
    {li if an operation removes the item without putting a path in its place
        ([with type t := …], [with module type S := …]), the reference keeps
        naming the original;}
    {li if the item is not copied, the reference keeps naming the
        original.}}

    A copy that is an alias, as [with module M = A] gives, has no page of its
    own; a reference to it is shown like any path to that alias.
    [module type of M] copies [M]'s own doc comment too, so that comment is
    shown, and its references resolved, in a second place.}}

(See {{!gaps}section 11}.)

{2:ref_failures 4.5 Reporting Failures}

A reference that does not resolve always gets an ordinary warning. It is
hand-written, so a missing root is as likely to be a typo as a missing
library.

A reference to a hidden unit, such as [{!Foo__Bar}], or to an item hidden
with [(**/**)], also gets a warning, and is not linked: its target has no
page or anchor to link to.

{1:display 5. Display}

What a name means and how it is shown are separate. [show(p)] picks how the
path [p] is printed and where its link goes. It never changes what [p]
refers to.

{ul
{li {b S1 (canonical).} A [@canonical] tag is a reference, resolved as in
    {{!refs}section 4}. A tag may name the library of its root, as in
    [@canonical /lib/Name.X]. If it resolves, paths to the module are shown
    as its target. [odoc] trusts the tag, and does not check that the target
    is the same module. A wrong tag can give links to anchors that do not
    exist. Canonical targets are often forward references: a library names
    as canonical a module of a library that depends on it, as eio does.

    A tag applies to paths through the module it is written on. A path that
    names the module directly, such as [X__M.t] where dune's tag is on the
    alias [X.M], is shown as it is (see
    {{!page-dune.library_wrapping}dune's library wrapping}).}
{li {b S2 (fallback).} If a canonical tag does not resolve, the path is
    shown as it is. A canonical that does not resolve is reported as in
    {{!ref_failures}section 4.5}. This includes a tag whose root is
    ambiguous.

    Known limit: dune's tags name no library, so if two libraries in [Λl]
    have the same main module, the tags are ambiguous (see
    {{!page-features.canonical_paths}Canonical Paths}).}
{li {b S3 (hidden).} Hidden units, such as [Foo__Bar], are not linked to,
    and do not appear in the search index or the sidebar.}}

{1:legacy 6. Legacy Units}

A legacy unit has no [Λc]. For it, [odoc] does what it did before units
recorded their libraries, and this page makes no promise about it beyond that.
A command that takes no [-L], such as [odoc html-url], knows no library
names, so it treats every unit as a legacy unit.
{{!claims}Section 3.7} does not cover roots whose origin is a legacy unit. How
legacy and recorded units mix is left to the implementation, which should do
whatever keeps the code simplest.

{1:operations 7. Operations}

Every operation [odoc] performs on names is listed here. Each must keep the
origin of every root occurrence in the terms it handles ({b O1}), and must
treat a reference bound by R1 as a local path (R4).

{t
  | Operation | When | Obligation |
  |-----------|------|------------|
  | Load: read the typed tree | compile | give each root its origin; record [Λc(u)] in the unit; R0 |
  | Resolve a root with a digest | compile or link | P1, P3 over [Λc(o)]; at compile, by D5; V1 may leave it for link |
  | Resolve a root with no digest | compile or link | P2, P3 over [Λc(o)]; at compile, it may be left for link, and must be if the target is not yet compiled |
  | Expand an alias to a hidden module | compile or link | O1, R4 |
  | Expand an alias to a self-canonical module | link | O1, R4 |
  | Recompute [include], [module type of], a module type path | compile or link | O1, R4; if it fails, warn ({{!roots}section 3.1}) |
  | Substitute: functor application, [with], [:=] | compile or link | O1, R4 |
  | Strengthen | compile or link | new roots take the origin of the path they come from |
  | Unresolve and resolve again ([Invalidated], opaque paths) | compile and link | keep the origin |
  | Cache a lookup or an expansion | compile and link | the key includes the origin of each unresolved root in it |
  | Convert between component and model forms | compile and link | keep the origin, and the R1 bindings |
  | Write and read a unit | compile and link | keep the origin, so that [Λc(o)] and [o]'s import table can be found at link ([odoc] keeps only [Λc(o)], {{!gaps}section 11}); keep the R1 bindings |
  | Resolve a reference | link | R2, R3, then projection, which follows {{!paths}section 3}; R4 |
  | Resolve a canonical tag | link | S1, S2 |
  | Find the implementation of a unit (source links, shapes) | link | the implementation of that name among the libraries of the unit being linked, where the shape is reduced from; there is at most one, as dune forbids two implementations of one virtual library in a closure; if there is none, no source link |
  | Check D6 | link | warn per missing library, for every unit whose paths are resolved |
}

A root that is unresolved after a write must not lose its origin. [odoc]
writes [Λc(o)] with each unresolved root.

{1:order 8. Order of the Rules}

+ A path: P1, or P2 if no digest; then P3, with V1.
+ A reference: R1 at compile (R0); then R2; then R3; then
  projection, where paths follow item 1; references that R4 turned into
  paths follow item 1 too.
+ Display: S1, S2 and S3, after meaning is fixed.

P-rules use [Λl] only to find the directory of each library in [Λc]. They
never take candidates from it. R2 and R3 never look at [Λc], except through
a path met during projection, or a reference that R4 turned into a path.

{1:evidence 9. Evidence}

The rules rely on facts about the OCaml compiler: how it binds a name with
two candidates, what a pure alias records, which names a unit's import table
holds, and what [include], [module type of] and an implementation compiled
against another library's [.cmi] record. The tests
in [test/xref2/scope] depend on these facts, so a compiler that behaves
otherwise makes them fail.


{1:questions 10. Open Questions}

- R2: should [odoc] take the compiler's [-open] flags into account? It could
  read them from the compiler's command line, which [.cmt] and [.cmti] files
  record ([cmt_args]), or the driver could pass them. Not for now.
- R4 and shadowed copies. If [S] declares [type t] and documents a value
  with [{!t}], then in [sig include S type t = int end] the reference names
  the included copy of [t], which is shadowed and not shown. The compiler
  allows this, because no type uses the shadowed [t]. Should the reference
  link to the new [t], to [S.t], or not at all?
- R4 and anonymous arguments. If a comment in the result of
  [F (X : sig type t end)] says [{!X.t}], then in
  [module R = F (struct type t = int end)] the application replaces [X] by
  an argument that has no path. Should the reference name [F]'s parameter,
  show [int], or not link? A module type applies functors only to paths,
  so in an [.mli] file this happens only inside [module type of], as in
  [module type of struct include F (struct type t = int end) end]. Today
  the question does not arise: [odoc] keeps the compiler's signature for
  [R], which has no comments at all
  ({{:https://github.com/ocaml/odoc/issues/1394}issue 1394}).

{1:gaps 11. Known Gaps}

These are the places where [odoc] does not yet do what the rules above
require. Each says what the rule requires, and what [odoc] does instead.

- {b V1 and R3 (copies).} A copy must be told apart from the virtual
  library's unit by the implementation next to it. [odoc] does not do this.
  At compile, a root that records the digest of a virtual module takes the
  unit with that digest from the last [-L] that holds one, with no warning,
  so the result depends on the order of the [-L] options. A root left for
  link takes [vl]'s unit. A reference whose candidates are [vl]'s unit and
  copies of it is ambiguous: [odoc] warns and takes [vl]'s unit.
  odoc_driver meets D9 only when it finds the implementation's source,
  because it runs [odoc compile-impl] only then. [odoc compile-impl] itself
  does not need the source.
- {b Recomputing signatures} ({{!roots}section 3.1}). When [odoc] keeps the
  compiler's signature, it must warn. [odoc] does not try to recompute
  [module type of] a functor application, and keeps the compiler's signature
  with no warning.
- {b R0 (binding at compile).} R1 must bind the first part of each reference
  when its unit is compiled. [odoc] does not: it resolves each reference at
  link, in the context of the page where the comment is shown.
- {b R2 on the wrapper's page.} A sibling reference [{!Bar}] in a dune
  wrapped library must not resolve (R2's known limit). [odoc] does resolve
  it on the wrapper's page, because the comment is looked up again there.
  This stops when R0 is implemented.
- {b R3 and the unit being linked.} The root of a reference or a canonical
  tag must be looked up among [units(Λl, N)], and is ambiguous if there are
  several. When [N] is the name of the unit being linked, [odoc] finds that
  unit alone, whatever else [Λl] holds. So a reference or a canonical tag
  written in another unit, even in another library, whose root has the
  linked unit's name resolves to the linked unit with no warning, where R3
  and S2 say it is ambiguous.
- {b Ambiguous names} (R1, R3, S2). A reference or a canonical tag whose
  first part has several candidates must stay unresolved, with a warning, except where R3 chooses
  between a virtual library's unit and its copies. [odoc] warns,
  and then takes one of the candidates.
- {b Digests no unit matches} ({{!path_failures}section 3.6}). Such a root
  must get an ordinary warning. [odoc] gives no warning, or, with
  [--enable-missing-root-warning], reports it as a missing root.
- {b References to hidden targets} ({{!ref_failures}section 4.5}). A
  reference to a hidden unit or a hidden item must get a warning. [odoc]
  gives none. It shows a reference to a hidden unit as unresolved, and links
  a reference to a hidden item to an anchor that is not on the page.
- {b Hidden units in the index} (S3). Hidden units must not appear in the
  search index or the sidebar. [odoc compile-index] and
  [odoc sidebar-generate] include each hidden unit they are given.
- {b Canonical tags that fail} ({{!ref_failures}section 4.5}). A misspelt
  tag, or one whose library is not given at link, must get a warning. [odoc]
  gives none.
- {b D7 at link.} A [-L] set that binds one library name to two directories
  breaks D7, and must be rejected. [odoc compile] rejects it; [odoc link]
  accepts it.
- {b R4 (copies).} Each operation must rebind the references that R1 bound,
  as R4 says. None does: references pass through the conversions between
  the internal forms unchanged, as model values, and only heading labels are
  rebound. Resolved references need a component form for this.
- {b S1 (canonical).} A [@canonical] tag must be resolved as a reference.
  [odoc] resolves it as a module path, whose root may name its library.
- {b Origins at link} ({{!roots}section 3.1}). A root with a digest that
  is left for link must still be looked up with [o]'s import table. [odoc]
  keeps only [Λc(o)] with each root, not [o]. At compile it uses the
  digests of the unit being compiled for every root, whatever its origin.
  At link it uses the digests of
  the unit being linked for a root whose scope is that unit's own, and looks
  up any other root by name in [Λc(o)], as if it were not in an import
  table at all. By D3 the lookup by name finds the
  unit the digest names ({{!claims}section 3.7}), so the meaning is the
  same. What is lost is the check: a root whose digest matches no unit in
  [Λc(o)], for example because a library was rebuilt after [o] was
  compiled, is not reported ({{!path_failures}section 3.6}).
